he same time they also engage in  economic coercion so put it all together  this is what they want to do to taiwan  when they want to invade taiwan  and all of this these exercises were a  response to nancy pelosi's visit here at  the beginning of august when you reflect  on that visit there was a lot of debate  about whether it was the right thing to  do  was it worth it for all of this  let me make a clarification  the chinese were claiming that speaker  pelosi's visit to taiwan  is something that they need to respond  to  but speaker pelosi's visit to taiwan is  just  one of those  strings of the visitors coming to taiwan  and there was  presidents for example newt gingrich  also visited taiwan before we have so  many senators and congressmen or senior  officials  visiting taiwan from other parts of the  world  and  from this you can see that china just  used this as a pretext to launch that  military threat against taiwan and what  i would say is that speaker pelosi is  special to taiwan it's a moral booster  for the chinese people  taiwan has been facing the threat from  china for all this time  and if there's anyone  want to show their support for taiwan he  or she is highly appreciated  and speaker pelosi is a notable american  leader and her willingness to come to  taiwan to show support to the chinese  people i think this is a great thing for  the taiwanese people  to see and the chinese people throughout  surveys or through real actions they  show their enthusiasm in welcoming the  speaker and after speakers visit to  taiwan even though with the chinese  military threat there are more people  who want to come to taiwan and show  their support and you're seeing some  visitors in taiwan at this time because  they want to show support to taiwan and  all these kinds of shows of support to  taiwan are highly appreciated  and  for the chinese claims i think we need  to clarify  that china just want to find the pretext  if they don't find the pretext of  speaker pelosi's visit to taiwan they  will find something else and they can  always find something else if they can  find speaker pelosi scheduled to taiwan  as an excuse to launch such a large  military scale uh exercises i think they  can find many more extra many more  excuses so that's just the chinese  authoritarian way of doing things  and let me tell you in a very frank way  the chinese military threat against  taiwan will not stop taiwan from making  more friends  and at the same time it will not stop  international friends to come to taiwan  and show their support to us  minister what is your assessment of  russia's invasion of ukraine people have  been calling it a wake up call for  taiwan what lessons are you drawing from  how the world is unfolding  uh this is a very interesting question  uh at the same time we see the war in  ukraine is still going on we also see  the chinese military trade against  taiwan increasing  over in the european theater  we saw that back in 2014  russia  took hold of crimea  and the world response was not enough to  stop russia  from going further  and now we end up a war  in ukraine  and in ukraine we saw that was truly  unfortunate that a democracy  or a southern country that was under  attack by an authoritarian regime  but the ukrainians were showing their  bravery  by defending their country defending  their democracy and the kinds of bravery  also attracted lots of international  support so that is inspirational to the  chinese people because we understand  that the chinese can also do the same  thing to taiwan and by that time we want  to show to the international community  that we are just about  the same degree of bravery in fighting  for our country fighting for our  democracy fighting for our people and  fighting for our democratic way of life  and in east asia if you look at the  longer span of political events  hong kong  is something that we need to keep in  mind as well  just a couple of years ago when the  chinese government was imposing the  national security law on hong kong to  take away every bit of freedom away from  the hong kong people  a lot of people around the world are  asking  is chinese government going to stop at  hong kong or who is the next and at that  time a lot of people are already talking  about taiwan might be the next and i  think the story can go further  is taiwan going to be the last target  of the chinese authoritarian expansion  i would say no  the reason is very clear china also  claims east china sea and they'll be  conducting military exercises or sending  their ships to the disputed water china  also claims south china sea and their  daily patrol of the south china sea  either by their bombers or by their  warships uh is uh so frequent uh these  days is china limited uh what is the  chinese ambition limited to east china  sea taiwan strait and the south china  sea i can tell you no if it's not  stopped  china already signed a security  agreement with the sonoma islands which  is very far away from china  and if we don't stop it  the chinese government is going to sign  more security agreements with more  pacific countries  remember  the chinese leader has already said the  pacific ocean is big enough to  accommodate china and the united states  that's how ambitious they are and are  they limited to the indo-pacific region  or the pacific area i would say no if  you look at the naval bases they are  building in the  cambodia  in myanmar  in pakistan  in sri lanka all the way to djibouti i  think they have global ambition  and the expansion of authoritarianism  always is at the cost of democracy and i  think it's a time for democracy to unite  to stop the authoritarianism from  expanding further mr wu though um i mean  how seriously is taiwan taking this  threat in military terms i mean  we've heard very recently taiwan is  increasing its defense budget by almost  14 from 2022 to 2023 but that still just  takes defense spending to 2.4 percent of  gdp if we compare that with the united  states it spends 3.7 percent israel  which faces maybe a similar sort of  existential threat  5.6  uh how how is it enough for taiwan to be  spending less than half of what israel  spends on its defense this is a very  good question  we understand that  the defense is not free  and it may cost a lot of money and  therefore if you look at the increase of  our monetary budget in the last few  years it's been increasing steadily and  other than increasing our monetary  budget we also conducted the reform of  our military strategy  for example we have already established  an all-out defense mobilization agency  responsible for designing how taiwan can  mobilize itself for the homeland defense  and we also acquire asymmetric weapons  and to discuss with the united states  how we can best approach  the strategy of asymmetric symmetricity  and this is the kind of things that we  are looking at  and we of course understand that the  more military budget is needed and other  than the regular military budget as you  saw from the press  we also have special budget uh that is  used for uh items like missiles or uh  jet planes so these are what we try to  do and we understand the importance of  this and the government is  determined to increase our monetary  budget so do you think the people of  taiwan need to kind of  anticipate maybe spending here needs to  hit the five percent of gdp kind of  range sort of comparable to israel well  if you look at the the increase of  taiwan's economy in the last few years  uh the kinds of increase that we have is  already substantial and what i can tell  you is that the government is determined  to continue to increase our monetary  budget  um at the same time of course taiwan is  very very dependent on the united states  for for its security in terms of  providing weapons and also the sort of  implicit uh  promise of support from the united  states but i want to talk about what  we've heard from president biden over  the last year or so  on three separate occasions joe biden  has  said yes the united states would defend  taiwan going beyond the official u.s  policy only for the white house press  team within 24 hours having to walk that  back and say oh no no nothing's changed  it's exactly the same  how does that how does that make you  feel this sense of complete confusion  about the us policy  um coming from the president himself  well we're not confused  uh we are very clear  that the defense of taiwan is our own  responsibility  and we also are very clear about what  the taiwan relationship says the taiwan  relations act says that the united  states is obligated to provide taiwan  with defensive articles  and the u.s has been living up to the  commitment  of the taiwan relations act and we  appreciate that very much  in the last few years  our relations with the biden  administration has been very well and  going very strong and we have been  discussing taiwan's defense need  the kinds of defense strategies that  taiwan will need to be able to defend  itself so we don't doubt on the u.s  commitment to taiwan it's been very  clear  and i need to tell you once again  the defense of taiwan is our own  responsibility if we are not committed  to our own defense  we don't have the right to ask others to  sacrifice and under these kinds of  circumstances we need to show that we  are determined to defend ourselves and  just like the case of ukraine it's only  the ukrainian people showed their  determination to defend themselves now  you can see the outpouring of  international support for ukraine  and that is what we are looking at too  yes but but i want to return to biden's  comments because you say you're not  confused but i'm confused and many of  people are confused about what why joe  biden  said that said something that we have  promised to go beyond what was really  promised how do you interpret that is he  creating a kind of  next level strategic ambiguity  uh is he having a senior moment is he on  top of things  how what's your interpretation well we  have been working together with the  biden administration as a whole  and we've been speaking to senior  officials and what the u.s policy is and  what the united states support will be  and this kind of a real situation has no  ambiguity to us and we understand that  the us is committed to providing weapons  to taiwan for taiwan self defense and  the u.s is also seriously engaging  taiwan for security discussions so these  are what the united states has been  providing and we  will continue to discuss with the united  states and what we would need and other  than that  you should probably also look at the u.s  presence in this region and the way they  show their presence in this region  just a couple of days ago you saw two  u.s ships setting through the  tama strait  that is the way the united states is  showing its presence in this region and  by doing that i think the u.s is showing  its commitment to peace and stability in  this region and it is not just the  united states that is doing that  we saw australia canada uk france and  etc they also sent their ships to this  region to conduct freedom of navigation  operations in line with their  indo-pacific strategy so it's not only  the united states that has a stake in  peace and stability in this area  it's a lot of countries that understand  the stake of peace and stability in this  area and they want to show that they  care about this by sending their ships  to this region minister there are around  two million taiwanese people living in  china and china is taiwan's largest  trading partner isn't your government  simply too dependent on the us putting  eggs in the same basket  well a lot of people are saying  different things  uh some people are saying that we are  too dependent on china and at the same  time there are also people criticizing  the taiwanese government for depending  solely on  the united states  but the real situation is like this you  know our relations with china is quite  complicated uh  on the same you know on one hand uh  china is our number one trading partner  and probably number one or number two uh  destination for our outbound investment  and that is the fact that we cannot deny  but at the same time china is also our  only source of threat  and that is also the fact that we have  we have to reckon with and when china is  threatening taiwan to such a degree  we have to look around and see who else  can provide taiwan with support and we  see the united states and we see other  democracies so this is what we can count  on if there's going to be more  hostility in between taiwan and china  we need to unite the federal democracies  to come to taiwan's support and i think  the support has been increasing  if you look at the statements of g7 or  nato summit  or  eu summit or other platforms of two plus  twos  you can see that there are more and more  discussions of taiwan issue they all  talk about the importance of peace and  stability over the taiwan straight  indirectly they are saying that chinese  military trade against taiwan is  unwelcomed so this is the situation we  are seeing we not only rely on the  united states we have more and more  support from fellow democracies now the  head of the main opposition party here  the guam dang eric jew we've been  speaking to him  he's been speaking to dw as well  he's accusing your government of i quote  putting taiwan in a dangerous situation  uh by not having sufficient dialogue not  attempting dialogue with mainland with  beijing  he says that the ukraine war shows that  taiwan needs to seek that dialogue what  do you say to that  well  i'm the minister of foreign fs i don't  want to get into a domestic political  debate  but the reality is like this  taiwanese government has been open to  dialogues with china  the president says that the government  officials have been repeating that as  long as china 